Incidentally, I played my first serious archeologist yesterday 
(played four or five as a pacifist trial a year ago or so) 
and ascended him wish/genoless; they're not so hard.  

Drop the pickaxe and get a dwarvish mattock.  Even the +4 does 
not really compensate the advantage of the mattock, since it's 
doing excellent damage against large creatures.  Yes, it's 
heavy, but it's worth it.  

Do some heavy-duty sacrificing; you will find an altar at 
the quest, but hopefully find some in the dungeons before that.  
(The quest ain't too hard, btw, you can go for it with AC -15, 
HP 140, I think.)  For artifacts, first hope for sabers, since 
you can become expert in saber.  You are restricted in long sword, 
which sucks, but this will change to 'basic max' once you get one 
as a gift.  Which suffices if you want to #twoweapon (which 
I always recommend), since you get this at basic too (but 
you get it, and that's great).  It's painful, but I recommend 
to search the whole mines for good gear, checking every pair 
of iron shoes, every hard hat and also the dwarvish chain mails.  
This usually gives you 2 to 4 additional AC.  

Also, try to get all of Croesus' money (and I mean all), 
and buy all protection you can get.  
 
> I've noticed that archaeologists get awfully short-changed when it
> comes to hit points, and don't do so well in melee unless you get one
> heck of an artifact weapon, or a heavily enchanted pickaxe of mattock.

The HP gain is the same as for knights or monks; you maybe 
were just unlucky.  Or your Con was too low for significant 
boni.  Increase hit points by the usual means (nurses, alchemy, 
golden glow).  

>  What's the smart way to progress with the delvers?  I'm reluctant to
> abandon the pick-axe for anything other than an artifact weapon, and
> no opportunities have come by.  His HP, while pretty steady and
> well-protected with -15 AC, makes me way about any potentially
> powerful creature.  And his spell points are... surprisingly high. 

My experience was that some spells, in particular magic 
mapping and identify, are extremely helpful.  For the other 
spells, you can only proceed to basic in the attack business, 
and unless you want to abandon the GoP (which I advise 
against, since gnomes only get 18/50 max Str, translating 
to +1 Hit/+3 Damage, compared to the +3/+6 you get for 
Str 18/**--25), you will find it very difficult to cast 
finger of death reliably.  Additionally, fireball and 
ice storm aren't the fun they are on the expert level, 
but just rays.  The only spell of interest in the healing 
business is cure sickness, but this is only important 
if it's relieable, i.e. at 0%; else use the blessed unicorn 
horn.  (I use it exclusively most of the time, sometimes 
a little sweat with Pestilence, but unless Demogorgon appears, 
no worries.)  There's no point to make any advances in the 
escape business unless you don't find a bag of holding and 
hence can't take a hundred wands of digging and teleport with 
you on the ascension run.  

Bottom line, I'd say an archaeologist is a fighter with 
good support from magic mapping and identify.  And you'll 
need those spell points when mapping out Gehennom, don't 
worry.  

